@page "/docs/cli"

<DocsPage Title="CLI" Description="Use the CLI to add components to your project.">
    <p>The CLI is a .NET tool on NuGet. <code>dnx</code>, which comes with the .NET 10 SDK, runs it without installing it, the way <code>npx</code> does for npm packages.</p>
    <CliCommand Args="--version" />
    <p>To pin a version for a repository instead, install it as a local tool. Then run it as <code>dotnet blazix</code>:</p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@ToolCode" Language="bash" />

    <DocsHeading Title="init" />
    <p>Use the <code>init</code> command to set up a new or existing Blazor project.</p>
    <p>The <code>init</code> command sets up Tailwind (without Node.js), adds the theme tokens and the usings, adds the <code>Blazix.Primitives</code> package and writes <a href="/docs/blazix-json"><code>blazix.json</code></a>. See <a href="/docs/installation#existing-project">Installation</a> for each change it makes.</p>
    <CliCommand Args="init" />
    <p><strong>Options</strong></p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@InitOptionsCode" Language="text" />
    <p>Running <code>init</code> again updates the build files and the vendored CSS. It never overwrites <code>Styles/theme.css</code> or <code>Styles/app.css</code>, which are yours.</p>

    <DocsHeading Title="add" />
    <p>Use the <code>add</code> command to add components to your project. It also adds the components they depend on.</p>
    <CliCommand Args="add [component...]" />
    <p><strong>Options</strong></p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@AddOptionsCode" Language="text" />
    <p>A file you already have is kept, unless you pass <code>--overwrite</code>.</p>

    <DocsHeading Title="migrate rtl" />
    <p>Use <code>migrate rtl</code> to make components you&apos;ve already added right-to-left ready, and set <code>"rtl": true</code> so later adds come that way. See <a href="/docs/rtl">RTL</a>.</p>
    <CliCommand Args="migrate rtl [path]" />
    <p><strong>Options</strong></p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@MigrateOptionsCode" Language="text" />
    <p>Run it once: a second run adds the <code>rtl:</code> classes again. Without a path, it migrates the files in <code>componentsDir</code>.</p>

    <DocsHeading Title="list" />
    <p>Use the <code>list</code> command to see the components you can add. The ones you&apos;ve added are marked.</p>
    <CliCommand Args="list" />
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private const string ToolCode = """
        dotnet new tool-manifest
        dotnet tool install Blazix.Cli
        dotnet blazix add button
        """;

    private const string InitOptionsCode = """
        Usage: blazix init [options]

        Options:
          -p, --project <path>   the project to set up, when the folder has more than one
          --dir <folder>         where components go (default: "Components/UI")
          --rtl                  copy components right-to-left ready ("rtl": true)
          --skip-package         don't add the Blazix.Primitives package
        """;

    private const string MigrateOptionsCode = """
        Usage: blazix migrate rtl [path] [options]

        Arguments:
          path                   a file or folder to migrate (default: componentsDir)

        Options:
          -p, --project <path>   the project, when the folder has more than one
          -y, --yes              migrate again when "rtl" is already true
        """;

    private const string AddOptionsCode = """
        Usage: blazix add <component...> [options]

        Options:
          -p, --project <path>   the project to add to, when the folder has more than one
          --overwrite            replace files you already have
          --skip-package         don't add the Blazix.Primitives package
        """;
}
